# Setting up a new host
# This flake uses flake-parts + import-tree (modules/parts.nix, flake.nix):
# every *.nix under modules/ is auto-imported, so a new file just has to
# define flake.nixosModules.<name> or flake.nixosConfigurations.<name> and
# it becomes available -- nothing to register by hand in flake.nix itself.
#
# Look at modules/systems/alaptop/ (real laptop, LUKS root) and
# modules/systems/vmtest/ (throwaway VM, whole-disk GPT) as the two
# reference hosts. Copy whichever is the closer shape.

1. Create modules/systems/<host>/<host>.nix
    - flake.nixosConfigurations.<host> = inputs.nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem { modules = [ ... ]; };
    - Always include: self.nixosModules.global, self.nixosModules.<host>Settings,
      self.nixosModules.<host>Disko, self.nixosModules.user-shot, self.nixosModules.sshd,
      self.nixosModules.customFirewall, { nixpkgs.hostPlatform = "x86_64-linux"; }
    - Add self.nixosModules.workstation for a full GUI desktop, plus whichever
      of communications/meeting/games/recording/creative/mullvad/hypridlesuspend/sunshine
      apply to this machine (see alaptop.nix for the full-workstation laptop example,
      vmtest.nix for a minimal headless-ish box that only adds sunshine).
    - flake.nixosModules.<host>Settings = { pkgs, ... }: { ... } holds the
      machine-specific bits:
        boot.loader.systemd-boot.enable / efi.canTouchEfiVariables
        boot.kernelModules / boot.initrd.kernelModules / boot.initrd.availableKernelModules
          (copy from the host whose hardware is closest, then fix up after
          `lspci`/`nixos-generate-config` on the real machine)
        networking.hostName, networking.networkmanager.enable
        var.monitors = [ { name = "..."; width = ...; height = ...; refresh = ...;
          x = ...; y = ...; scale = ...; primary = true; } ... ]
          -- output names are placeholders until you can run `wlr-randr` on the
          booted host; this same list drives both mango's monitor layout and the
          per-monitor wallpaper crop (modules/theme/wallpaper.nix), so there's
          nothing else to configure for wallpaper.
        var.firewall.untrustedInterfaces.<iface> = { ... } if this host has an
          interface that should default-deny (see firewall.nix + alaptop/vmtest
          for the ipGroups/generalAllowed*Ports shape)
        environment.systemPackages = [ pkgs.vim ] (rescue editor before the
          terminal module pulls in neovim)
        home-manager.users.shot.home.stateVersion / system.stateVersion --
          pin to the release this repo currently targets (currently "26.05",
          see flake.nix's nixpkgs input), never bump on later hosts without
          also bumping the existing ones

2. Create modules/systems/<host>/disko.nix
    - flake.nixosModules.<host>Disko = { ... }: { imports = [ inputs.disko.nixosModules.disko ]; ... }
    - fileSystems."/nix".neededForBoot / "/persistent".neededForBoot = true
    - disko.devices.nodev."/" = tmpfs root (both existing hosts use an
      impermanence-style tmpfs root with /persistent + /nix as real subvolumes)
    - Two disk layouts to choose from:
        Whole disk, disko partitions it (vmtest-style): disko.devices.disk.main
          with content.type = "gpt" and boot/esp/swap/root partitions defined
          inline. Good for VMs or a fresh drive disko can wipe.
        Pre-partitioned / LUKS (alaptop-style): disko.devices.disk.esp/swap/root
          point at fixed existing device paths (/dev/nvme0n1pN) and disko only
          formats+mounts, it doesn't partition. Use this when dual-booting or
          when the partition table already exists; partition by hand first
          (parted/fdisk/gdisk) to match the device paths you reference, and
          for LUKS set content.type = "luks" with a passwordFile (disko-install
          reads /tmp/luks.txt -- have that file in place, e.g. from an
          interactive `read` prompt, before running disko-install).
    - btrfs subvolumes in both: /persistent, /nix, /tmp (+ /var/log on alaptop)

3. Install
    - Boot a NixOS installer with network + this repo reachable (clone it, or
      have it already checked out).
    - install.sh is a one-liner: adjust `--flake .#<host>` and `--disk <device>`
      (only meaningful for the whole-disk/disko-partitions case) and run it --
      it shells out to `disko-install` against the flake's nixosConfigurations.<host>.
    - For the pre-partitioned/LUKS case, partition the disk yourself first and
      make sure the LUKS password file disko-install expects is in place, then
      run disko-install the same way.
    - SSH host keys are generated automatically by openssh on first boot and
      then kept across reboots by preservation (sshd.nix preserves
      /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*); nothing to pre-seed. If this host needs to accept
      SSH from a particular pubkey, add it to
      modules/programs/services/sshd.nix's users.users.shot.openssh.authorizedKeys.keys.

4. First boot / manual sign-ins
    - Run post_install.sh -- it walks through everything that can't be
      declared in Nix: adding the GitHub SSH key, tailscale login, Bitwarden
      security-key registration (rbw register), enabling Firefox extensions,
      and signing into Mullvad / Vesktop / RadarOmega / Steam / Heroic /
      Signal / Prism Launcher. Follow it top to bottom.
    - Firefox: the extension list is declared (firefox.nix), but the
      tridactyl *config* lives at the repo root (./tridactyl) and isn't wired
      up through home-manager -- copy it to ~/.config/tridactyl/tridactylrc
      by hand.
    - Games: Steam -> Settings -> Storage -> add ~/Games/Steam as default;
      Heroic -> Settings -> Game Defaults -> Advanced -> wrapper command `gmmh`.

5. Ongoing rebuilds
    - Once terminal.nix's modules are active, use `nh os switch` (wraps
      nixos-rebuild, adds generation cleanup per modules/programs/terminal/nh.nix)
      instead of nixos-rebuild directly.

6. Template stub
    - template.nix defines flake.nixosModules.template, a blank
      environment.systemPackages / home.packages module meant as a scratch
      starting point -- it isn't imported by any host by default. Copy its
      shape (or just add self.nixosModules.template to a host's module list)
      if you want a place to stash host-specific one-off packages instead of
      growing <host>Settings.
